\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Projected Gaussian laws and the massless limit}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Use the \vocabk{kernel decomposition}{fdip-0031} of a
self-adjoint positive semidefinite #{L:E\to E} on a finite real
inner-product space #{(E,h)}. Let #{\nu_m} be the projection onto
#{H} of the law with precision #{Q_m=L+m^2I},
#{m>0}. Then #{\nu_m} converges to the constrained law
#{\nu_H} of \ref{fdip-000C} in total variation, meaning
#{\sup_{A\text{ Borel}}|\nu_m(A)-\nu_H(A)|\to0}.
For every fixed polynomial #{P} on #{H}, its expectations converge too.}

\p{For a fixed source #{j\in E} and #{\ell_j(x)=h(j,x)},
the variance satisfies
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}_{Q_m}(\ell_j)
 &=m^{-2}\|P_Kj\|^2\\
 &\quad+h_H\bigl(p_Hj,(L_H+m^2I_H)^{-1}p_Hj\bigr).
\end{aligned}}
Here #{p_H:E\to H} is the orthogonal projection with codomain #{H},
#{h_H} is its induced pairing, and #{P_H=ip_H:E\to E}.
The variance has a finite limit if and only if #{P_Kj=0},
equivalently #{j\in H}; in that case its limit is #{h(j,L^+j)}.}

\p{When #{L} is the \vocabk{weighted graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007}
of a connected nonempty graph with positive edge weights, put
#{n=|V|}. The source #{u_0=\mathbf1_V/\sqrt n} has variance
#{m^{-2}}. Its contribution to every Green-kernel entry is
#{1/(nm^2)}, and #{G_m(v,v)\geq 1/(nm^2)}. The variance of a field
difference converges to the constrained variance. On a disconnected
graph this holds for vertices in the same component; a difference
between components has a nonzero kernel projection.}
\proof{
\p{By \ref{fdip-0033}, the densities on #{H} are
#{f_m=w_m/Z_{H,m}}, where
##{\begin{aligned}
w_m(x)&=e^{-h(x,(L_H+m^2I_H)x)/2},\\
w_0(x)&=e^{-h(x,L_Hx)/2}.
\end{aligned}}
They are continuous, #{w_m\to w_0} pointwise, and
#{0\leq w_m\leq w_0}, with #{\int_H w_0=Z_H<\infty}.
Dominated convergence gives #{Z_{H,m}\to Z_H>0}.
For sufficiently small #{m}, #{Z_{H,m}\geq Z_H/2}; hence
##{|f_m-f_0|\leq 3w_0/Z_H.}
Another application gives #{\int_H|f_m-f_0|\to0}. For each Borel #{A},
#{|\nu_m(A)-\nu_H(A)|\leq\int_H|f_m-f_0|}, proving the uniform claim.
For a fixed polynomial, the dominating function
#{3|P|w_0/Z_H} is integrable by \ref{fdip-002W},
so #{\int P f_m\to\int P f_0}. Each use of dominated convergence
applies along every positive sequence #{m_j\to0}; the sequential
criterion gives the real-parameter limits as #{m\downarrow0}.
When #{H=0}, these laws are all the same point mass.}
\p{The inverse-covariance formula \ref{fdip-000B} and the
compressed inverse in \ref{fdip-0033} give the displayed variance.
To recover its spectral expression, choose an orthonormal eigenbasis
#{u_a} of #{L}, with corresponding eigenvalues #{\lambda_a\ge0}.
For #{\lambda_a>0}, let #{\widetilde u_a\in H} denote the vector
with #{i\widetilde u_a=u_a}. These vectors form an orthonormal
eigenbasis of #{L_H}. Orthogonal projection gives
##{h_H(p_Hj,\widetilde u_a)=h(j,i\widetilde u_a)=h(j,u_a).}
The inverse of #{L_H+m^2I_H} acts on #{\widetilde u_a} by
#{(\lambda_a+m^2)^{-1}}. Expanding #{p_Hj} therefore gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&h_H\bigl(p_Hj,(L_H+m^2I_H)^{-1}p_Hj\bigr)\\
&\qquad=\sum_{\lambda_a>0}
       \frac{h(j,u_a)^2}{\lambda_a+m^2}.
\end{aligned}}
Every term is nonnegative. The sum converges
as #{m\downarrow0}; the kernel term has a finite limit precisely
when #{P_Kj=0}. In that case the sum tends to #{h(j,L^+j)} by
\ref{fdip-0032}. If #{H=0}, the sum and compressed term are both zero.}
\p{For the connected nonempty graph, #{K} is spanned by the unit
constant vector #{u_0}. Its precision eigenvalue is #{m^2}, so its
source variance is #{m^{-2}}. The projection onto this direction
has kernel entries #{1/n}; multiplying by #{m^{-2}} gives the
constant contribution #{1/(nm^2)}. The other spectral contributions
to a diagonal entry are nonnegative, proving the diagonal bound.
The source of a field difference is #{\delta_v-\delta_w}.
It lies in #{H} when the vertices are in the same component, so the
variance criterion gives its constrained limit. For different
components its sum on either of those components is nonzero,
so its projection onto the component-constant kernel is nonzero.}
}
\p{These observable limits do not supply a normalized full-space
massless density; the constrained law in \ref{fdip-000C} uses its
own induced volume. The analytic ingredient is
\citet{Theorem 1.4.49, pp. 111-112, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
The three dominators in the proof verify its hypotheses for these
Gaussian laws and their fixed polynomial observables.
Mathlib's \leanref{MeasureTheory.tendsto_integral_of_dominated_convergence}
supports each sequential integral limit under the stated measurability,
integrable-majorant and almost-everywhere convergence hypotheses;
the density and variance identifications are proved here.}
